# Slugs that have been renamed. One hop, never a chain: every entry points
# at the guide's current URL, not at the slug that replaced it.

# NO TRAILING-SLASH NORMALISATION RULES. This is deliberate.
#
# There used to be three:
#
#   /guides/:slug  /guides/:slug/  301!
#   /topics/:slug  /topics/:slug/  301!
#   /ages/:slug    /ages/:slug/    301!
#
# They took every guide, topic and age page off the site with
# ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS. Two things combined to do it.
#
# Netlify matches a request for /topics/feeding/ against the source
# /topics/:slug — the trailing slash does not stop it matching — so the
# rule sends the URL to the address it already asked for. And the !
# makes the redirect FORCED, which beats a real file at that path, so
# the generated page existing changed nothing.
#
# Writing the sources out one at a time does NOT fix it: an explicit
# /guides/teething -> /guides/teething/ loops for the same reason. Any
# rule shaped /x -> /x/ is a loop here.
#
# Nothing is needed in their place. Netlify resolves /topics/feeding/
# to topics/feeding/index.html by itself, and serves the same file for
# the slashless form. Ordinary directory-index behaviour.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# AGE RANGES THAT ARE SWITCHED OFF
#
# Studio -> Site -> Ages. The guides below
# are tagged only to bands that are currently off. Nothing has been deleted:
# their Firestore documents, ages, slugs and illustrations are all intact,
# and switching the band back on republishes them at these same URLs.
#
# While they are off their addresses must not resolve, or the one thing
# hiding them was for — nobody reading them yet — fails for anyone holding
# the link. A real 404 is the honest answer: it is what the site already
# does for an address that is not published, it keeps them out of the
# index without naming them in robots.txt, and it is what makes Google
# drop any that were indexed before.
#
# These sit ABOVE the catch-all below, which would otherwise rewrite them
# to guide.html and render them from Firestore. First match wins.
# (none — every age range is currently public)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Slugs this build did not generate — must stay last.
#
# Not forced, so Netlify serves a real generated file whenever one exists
# and only falls through to here when it does not. Two things reach this
# line, and they need opposite treatment:
#
#   1. A guide added in Studio since the last deploy. It exists in
#      Firestore but has no page yet. It must render.
#   2. A slug that genuinely does not exist. It must not be indexed.
#
# Netlify cannot tell them apart — it does not know what is in Firestore.
# So this rewrites to guide.html, which does know: it reads the slug from
# the path and looks it up. Found, it renders the guide. Not found, it
# writes a noindex tag and shows the not-found message, so a dead URL
# still cannot end up in the index.
#
# This is the one place the architecture accepts a soft 404 rather than a
# hard one. The alternative was new guides 404ing until the next deploy,
# which is a far worse failure and one that happens far more often.
/guides/*  /guide.html  200
